Marked as "Acknowledged" by the client. The client provided the following explanation:

> The existence of non-standard behavior is reasoning behind the opt-out mechanism. We can’t make blanket judgement calls about what is and isn’t bridgeable as there will inevitably be some variance in business logic even among contracts that share standards. The basis for allowing projects to opt-out is to enable them to make that call for themselves in the interest of their project and their end users.

**Description:** The permissionless nature of this bridge lets any user onboard a non-already onboarded asset. While handlers can be used to handle tokens with a non-standard behavior we want to highlight the fact that multiple non-standard behaviors can exist, since tokens can:

*   be paused;
*   be upgraded;
*   blacklist/whitelist specific addresses;
*   have a fee mechanism;
*   have a rebasing mechanism;
*   have a hook mechanism when performing a transfer, which can lead to reentrancies (ex: `ERC777` standard);

This mainly matters on the native side of the bridged asset, since the bridged side will be represented by a standard template contract.

As a concrete example for fee-on-transfer tokens, when native EVM assets are escrowed, they are transferred to the Bridge COA, and an equal amount is minted on the other side. Accounting errors can arise if the transfer function levies fees and thus the amount received by the COA wallet is less than expected. In this case there are more tokens minted on the other side than escrowed on the native side. This may further lead to the Bridge unexpectedly running out of funds on the native side for bridging operations in that direction. This issue is further amplified by the public access to the onboarding functions and the inability to remove or blacklist incompatible token implementations.

**Recommendation:** Consider documenting the fact that users should verify the code of the asset they intend to bridge on both sides

