## Description
There are 2 checks non-defined by EIP-20 which are performed:

1. **Self transfer is prohibited:**

    ```solidity
    function transferFromInternal(address account, address from, address to, Shares shares) private returns (bool) {
        if (from == to) revert E_SelfTransfer(); // <<<
        decreaseAllowance(from, account, shares);
        transferBalance(from, to, shares);
        return true;
    }
    ```

2. **Self approve is prohibited:**

    ```solidity
    function setAllowance(address owner, address spender, uint256 amount) internal {
        if (spender == owner) revert E_SelfApproval(); // <<<
        vaultStorage.users[owner].eTokenAllowance[spender] = amount;
        emit Approval(owner, spender, amount);
    }
    ```

At least in the 2nd case, there is no point to Euler in banning self approval. On the contrary, these requirements can introduce DoS in integration protocols where, for example, a contract always gives approval to a spender and for some reason becomes the spender. According to that requirement, the contract becomes subject to DoS.

## Recommendation
Allow self transfer and self approval to reduce potential issues in integration.

